hello again welcome to another episode  of community questions that i'm allowed  to host i've decided to move the format  around a little bit all these questions  are in some way about regularization  techniques so instead of going through  them rather randomly i'll try to explain  things a bit more oddly feel free to  skip to either part using the timestamps  below  so what is regularization and yes  everyone who writes it with a zed is a  bloody savage sorry leticia a  regularization is any technique that  helps your neural network to solve your  objective function in a better way  put simply your objective or loss  function tells your net what to do and  regularization is added to tell it how  it should do that as a simple example  consider fitting a function through  these given points  while the red curve does hit every  sample point it generalizes badly for  the samples not seen during training it  is overfitting  a regularization term here could enforce  smoothness giving you that nice green  curve that you can see  usually these regularizations are added  as an extra term to the loss function  so two very common regularizations that  are already built in in most optimizers  such as atom would be l1 and taken off  regularization that is also known as l2  or weight decay l1 regularization just  adds the absolute sum of all weights of  the network to the loss term whereas  ticking off or l2 regularization does  the same for the squared weights in  practice that just means that l1 does  not care which weights we actually touch  as long as we are always getting lower  in total for l2 regularization this  changes as we square each weight this is  always punishing the highest weight the  most  so while l2 regularization and tknoff  mean the same l2 regularization shrinks  the weights more evenly than l1  preferring to punish the higher weights  more than the lower ones and l2 does  this in a linear way always just  preferring to get the total sum of the  weights down if we reconsider our  example from the beginning we can easily  see why having either regularization  term is helpful to form a curve such as  the red line we'd need much higher  weights very informally spoken please  don't hit me for that sharper turns of  the output function require larger  weights whereas for example a flat line  can be produced just with all weights  being zero so this also helps against  overfitting while improving  generalization by preferring the green  over the red line  easy solutions just tend to generalize  better than memorizing every last bit of  the training data  now let's look at one regularization for  the analysis lovers  let's quickly repeat what lipschitz  continuity is and see how we can use  that to for example better train guns or  defend against adversarial attacks  a function is lip shit's continuous with  the lipstick's constant c if a change  applied to its input value never changes  the output value by more than c times  that input change or with other words  there is some upper limit to the slope  of this function  as our networks are also just piecewise  linear functions we can also compute the  lipschitz constant for them discretely  spoken this means that for any two  samples of the input their output  distance relative to the input distance  should at most be that lipschitz  constant meaning for a network that is  lipschitz continuous when we fiddle  around with one single input the output  change occurring has some upper bound  and that is already the answer to this  question  a simple way of enforcing lipschitz  continuity is the so-called weight  clipping the output of a network always  depends on its weights and when we  simply set an upper limit to the  absolute value of a weight that is we  just clip any weight that is larger than  this limit we already obtain a very  crude limit on how much the change in  the input can change the output leaving  us with a lipschitz bound it's just the  same argument as before for the l1 and  l2 regularization high slope needs  higher values as coefficient meaning  high weights in the network  and by enforcing that our weights are  small we make sure that the learned  function does not have any of these  sharp turns that we do not want  now let's have a look at what else this  is good for imagine that you want to  train a gun and i'm explicitly saying  imagine here because these things are a  pain to drain  so you train your gun and during  training your discriminator becomes way  too good for the generator that means  that you suddenly have a strong jump in  your discriminator and meaning that when  your generator is producing superfake  outputs slightly changing something as  you'd usually do when training networks  won't help at all your output will still  be very much fake  hence you basically end up with a  vanishing gradient problem there is just  no clear direction in which your  gradient points that can help you you'd  need to change a whole lot of things and  then suddenly the discriminator gets to  this jump and thinks your generated  output is real  and this of course means that you can't  properly train your generator anymore  big problem lots of headache  so what do you do about that well you  don't want these jumps in your loss  function you want your loss function to  smoothly change with a limited rate of  change which is exactly what a lipschitz  bound for your network does so for  example you enforce lipshit's continuity  through weight clipping and boom things  get better  mostly  sometimes guns are lots of fun now  before you run off and use that  knowledge don't because there is an even  better regularization technique  lipschitz continuity prevents you from  having steep changes in the loss but it  does not prevent local optima or the  function changing only slowly or not at  all much better than that we can simply  say that our network should not be  lipschitz bound but instead also exactly  change by some constant relative to the  input change meaning our regularization  term is just enforcing a constant  gradient for example one everywhere  which in theory means that there is no  local optimum anymore a function into a  network with a constant gradient like  one everywhere has not one spot where  the gradient is zero it is just some  linear function and as we all know a  local optimum has always a gradient of  zero and on such a linear function we  can train even better lastly lipschitz  regularization can also help us against  adversarial attacks  for adversarial attacks we usually look  for a tiny shift in the input that  causes a large shift in the output for  example when we add almost invisible  noise perturbations to this image of a  panda we can make it look like a given  to a classifier that we trained before  well if only there was such a thing to  make sure these tiny input changes do  not totally change the output well again  lipschitz regularization to the rescue  enforcing a somewhat low lipschitz  constant for the network means that for  an input that is only slightly changed  we cannot change the output by much or  in other words we need to shift a lot  more pixels much more visibly to change  this panda into a gibbon in the eyes of  our classifier  so i hope that this was somewhat useful  to you as always i would be very happy  for some feedback in the comments and  we'll leave you some additional links  and sources in the comments bye bye
 

